<title>BELGIUM: EU's Fischler seeks global food safety rules.</title>
<headline>EU's Fischler seeks global food safety rules.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1996-10-01</dateline>
<text>
<p>The European Union should insist on world-wide animal health and food safety rules in the next round of world farm trade negotiations, EU Farm Commissioner Franz Fischler said on Monday.</p>
<p>Global rules are also needed for gene altered farm products and to take account of the environmental and social impact of farming, Fischler told the Konrad Adenauer Foundation, a German think-tank.</p>
<p>&quot;I'm saying that because I don't want to go into the next WTO (World Trade Organisation) negotiations with my back against the wall as my predecessor had to in the (GATT) Uruguay Round,&quot; Fischler said.</p>
<p>Fischler said he did not want to be forced into creating uncertainty for farmers through further radical reforms.</p>
<p>But he added that the EU could not adopt a &quot;wait and see&quot; attitude in order to avoid conflict with farmers as this would not help to solve future problems.</p>
<p>The next round of world farm trade talks is due to start in 1999.</p>
